("Renal 1","A 600 kg horse. About how much INTERSTITIAL fluid does it have?",["30 L","90 L","120 L","240 L"],[1],"Water 60% = 360 L · extracellular ⅓ = 120 L · interstitial ≈ ¾ of extracellular = 90 L (plasma ≈ 30 L)"),
("Renal 1","A dog gets a large IV volume of 5% dextrose (free water once the glucose is used). Once water finishes moving, which is true?",["Both compartments expand and both become more dilute","Only the extracellular compartment expands","Cells shrink as water moves out of them","Extracellular volume rises and osmolarity rises"],[0],"Free water spreads into BOTH compartments (no salt to hold it outside) → both bigger, both more dilute"),
("Renal 1","Which statement about the hole-in-the-bucket model is INCORRECT?",["Output rises as the water level rises","It eventually reaches a steady state","It settles at a level that depends on input","It maintains homeostasis at a set point"],[3],"No sensor, no set point → steady state but NOT homeostasis"),
("Renal 2","Which change would MOST increase net filtration at the START of the glomerular capillary?",["Doubling Bowman's space hydrostatic pressure","Raising plasma albumin concentration","Constricting the afferent arteriole","Constricting the efferent arteriole"],[3],"Efferent constriction ↑ glomerular hydrostatic pressure · the other three all reduce filtration"),
("Renal 2","Which are true of the fused glomerular basement membrane?"+SA,["Basel calls it the real filter","It carries a negative charge","It is produced by mesangial cells","It repels negatively charged albumin","Positive molecules cross it more easily"],[0,1,3,4],"Mesangial cells are support only · the membrane is negative, so it repels albumin and favors cations"),
("Renal 2","Which would be filtered, but at a LOWER concentration in the filtrate than in plasma?",["Inulin (5 kDa)","Glucose (0.18 kDa)","Myoglobin (17 kDa)","Albumin (66.5 kDa)"],[2],"Basel: <15 kDa = equal · 15–70 kDa = partial (>0 but lower) · albumin essentially none (negative)"),
("Renal 2","Under anesthesia a dog's mean arterial pressure falls to 60 mmHg. Best description of the kidney?",["Autoregulation holds GFR constant","The afferent is maximally dilated, so GFR falls","The efferent dilates to restore GFR","The macula densa constricts the afferent"],[1],"Autoregulation works ~80–170 · below 80 the afferent is already wide open"),
("Renal 2","Renal plasma flow falls by half while glomerular pressure is held constant. What happens?",["GFR falls; filtration fraction rises","GFR rises; filtration fraction falls","GFR and filtration fraction both fall","GFR and filtration fraction both rise"],[0],"Fewer 'sips' per minute (GFR ↓), but each sip is filtered harder (filtration fraction ↑)"),
("Renal 2","Which statement about the efferent arteriole is INCORRECT?",["It is an arteriole rather than a venule","Angiotensin II constricts it","It is the main site of myogenic autoregulation","Juxtamedullary efferents give rise to the vasa recta"],[2],"Myogenic autoregulation is mostly the AFFERENT arteriole"),
("Renal 3","A dog on carprofen (an NSAID) becomes hypotensive under anesthesia and is azotemic the next day. Best explanation?",["Carprofen blocked prostaglandin dilation of the afferent arteriole","Carprofen blocked ENaC, causing severe sodium loss","Carprofen constricted the efferent, raising glomerular pressure","Carprofen damaged the glomerular basement membrane"],[0],"Hypotension → kidney relies on prostaglandin E2 to keep the afferent open → NSAID removes it → GFR + O₂ crash"),
# ---- Renal 3
("Renal 3","Why is BUN a poor marker of GFR?"+SA,["Urea is partly reabsorbed","Urea is partly secreted","Its level changes with dietary protein","Its level changes with hydration","Urea is too large to be filtered"],[0,1,2,3],"Urea is freely filtered, but reabsorption, secretion, diet and hydration all change BUN"),
("Renal 3","Which GFR marker is freely filtered, then reabsorbed AND destroyed in the proximal tubule?",["Creatinine","SDMA","Cystatin C","BUN"],[2],"Cystatin C never returns to blood, so its blood level tracks filtration"),
("Renal 3","The renal clearance of drug X is much HIGHER than GFR. The most likely explanation is that X is:",["Filtered and partly reabsorbed","Filtered and also secreted","Bound to albumin and not filtered","Filtered with no further handling"],[1],"Clearance = GFR only if not reabsorbed or secreted · secretion adds extra removal → clearance > GFR"),
("Renal 3","Which does ANP do?"+SA,["Suppresses renin release","Suppresses aldosterone","Suppresses ADH release","Inhibits ENaC in the collecting duct","Stimulates NKCC in the thick ascending limb"],[0,1,2,3],"ANP shuts down everything that keeps salt and water"),
("Renal 3","A dog vomited for 2 days. BUN 70 (7–27), creatinine 1.4 (0.5–1.5), USG 1.045. Most likely?",["Chronic kidney disease","Acute kidney injury","Postrenal obstruction","Prerenal azotemia"],[3],"BUN ↑ with normal creatinine + highly concentrated urine = the kidney works; it's just dry"),
("Renal 3","Why can efferent constriction put the MEDULLA at risk of hypoxia?",["It lowers glomerular pressure, so filtration stops","It reduces downstream flow while raising sodium workload","It raises medullary flow, washing out the gradient","It triggers afferent constriction through feedback"],[1],"Less blood (O₂) reaches the medulla while more filtered Na⁺ must be pumped (O₂ demand ↑)"),
# ---- Renal 4
("Renal 4","Which proximal tubule transporter uses ATP directly?",["SGLT2","The Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger","The basolateral Na⁺/K⁺ pump","NaPi-IIa"],[2],"Basel: the proximal tubule spends ATP on ONE pump · everything else is secondary active"),
("Renal 4","Why does the lumen of the LATE proximal tubule become positive?",["K⁺ leaks back into the lumen through ROMK","Cl⁻ concentrates and leaks out between cells","Na⁺ is pumped into the lumen by the Na⁺/K⁺ pump","H⁺ is secreted by the Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger"],[1],"Lumen-positive #1 · (ROMK back-leak = #2, thick ascending limb)"),
("Renal 4","Which drug class is concentrated in proximal tubule cells by FILTRATE-side endocytosis?",["Aminoglycosides (gentamicin)","Penicillins","Tetracyclines","NSAIDs"],[0],"Megalin/cubam swallow filtered aminoglycosides · tetracyclines/penicillins enter by OAT from the blood side"),
("Renal 4","Expected effects of acetazolamide?"+SA,["Bicarbonate in the urine","Metabolic acidosis","Alkaline urine","Mild diuresis","Hypercalcemia"],[0,1,2,3],"Blocks carbonic anhydrase → bicarbonate not reclaimed → lost in urine → blood acidic, urine alkaline"),
("Renal 4","On a slide you see pale cells with clear borders bulging into a wide, clean lumen, and the tubule branches. What is it?",["Proximal tubule","Distal tubule","Thick ascending limb","Collecting duct"],[3],"Only collecting ducts (and vessels) branch · proximal = tall, pink, messy lumen"),
("Renal 4","Which statement about the proximal tubule is INCORRECT?",["It reabsorbs about 65% of filtered sodium","Its filtrate stays isosmotic to plasma","It extends into the inner medulla","It reclaims nearly all filtered bicarbonate"],[2],"The proximal tubule never enters the medulla (its straight part is in the medullary ray)"),
# ---- Renal 5-6
("Renal 5","Filtrate osmolarity at the end of the proximal tubule, the tip of a long loop, and the end of the distal tubule is about:",["300 · 1200 · 100","300 · 600 · 300","100 · 1200 · 300","300 · 1200 · 300"],[0],"Slide 19: iso → hyper at the tip → most hypotonic at the distal tubule"),
("Renal 5","Which segment is water-impermeable AND contains PTH-regulated active calcium entry?",["Proximal tubule","Thin ascending limb","Distal convoluted tubule","Thick ascending limb"],[2],"ECaC + calbindin + NCX/PMCA · the thick ascending limb is waterproof too, but its Ca²⁺ is passive"),
("Renal 5","A racehorse receives furosemide before every race. Which disturbances are expected over time?"+SA,["Hypokalemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypomagnesemia","Hypernatremia","Hyperkalemia"],[0,1,2],"Blocking NKCC wastes Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ (less lumen-positive charge)"),
("Renal 5","Which statement about aquaporins in principal cells is INCORRECT?",["ADH moves aquaporin-2 to the apical membrane","Basolateral aquaporins are always present","ADH adds aquaporins to the basolateral membrane","Without ADH the urine stays dilute"],[2],"Apical = ADH Adds · Basolateral = Built-in"),
("Renal 5","A cow develops chronic respiratory acidosis. Over time, its collecting duct shifts toward:",["More beta cells and alkaline urine","More principal cells and dilute urine","More alpha cells and acidic urine","More beta cells and acidic urine"],[2],"Kidney compensates by switching toward alpha cells (acid out, new bicarbonate in)"),
("Renal 6","Which is NOT required for countercurrent multiplication?",["Active NaCl transport by NKCC","A water-permeable descending limb","Active sodium pumping by the vasa recta","Opposite flow in the two loop limbs"],[2],"The vasa recta is PASSIVE (exchange) · it preserves but doesn't build the gradient"),
("Renal 6","A dog is in a water diuresis (low ADH). What happens to medullary urea?",["It rises, because UT-A1/A3 increase","It falls, because UT-A1/A3 decrease","It rises, because NKCC increases","It is unchanged; urea isn't regulated"],[1],"Low ADH → fewer urea transporters → less urea recycling → weaker gradient"),
("Renal 6","Which statement about urea is INCORRECT?",["It is freely filtered","It is always transported passively","It is pumped actively into the medulla","Its recycling is increased by ADH"],[2],"Urea is always passive; ADH just opens the transporters"),
# ---- Renal 7
("Renal 7","Per slide 19, which would cause a diuresis?"+SA,["Raising filtrate osmolarity","Lowering medullary osmolarity","Removing apical aquaporins","Inserting apical aquaporins","Increasing UT-A1/A3"],[0,1,2],"Shrink the filtrate–medulla difference or take away water's pathway → diuresis"),
("Renal 7","When hypothalamic osmoreceptor neurons shrink, which channel opens to trigger ADH release?",["TRPV1","TRPV4","TRPV5 (ECaC)","ROMK"],[0],"TRPV4 = flow sensor for BK · TRPV5 = distal calcium channel"),
("Renal 7","Angiotensin II stimulates the release of which hormones?"+SA,["Aldosterone","ADH","ANP","PTH","Calcitonin"],[0,1],"Salt (aldosterone) + water (ADH) together → volume ↑"),
("Renal 7","A dog with PU/PD has USG 1.004. After supervised water deprivation, USG is 1.030. Best plan?",["Start desmopressin twice daily","Start a thiazide with free water","Gradually restrict water intake","Start an ACE inhibitor"],[2],"Concentrates on its own = kidney and ADH work = primary polydipsia"),
("Renal 7","Why does uncontrolled diabetes mellitus cause polyuria?",["High glucose blocks ADH release","Glucose in the filtrate narrows the osmotic gradient","High glucose damages the collecting duct","Insulin deficiency blocks NKCC"],[1],"Above ~350 mg/dL, SGLTs saturate → glucose acts as an osmolyte in the filtrate"),
("Renal 7","An acidotic dog needs volume replacement. Which fluid is the best choice?",["0.9% NaCl","Lactated Ringer's","Hypertonic saline","Mannitol"],[1],"0.9% NaCl adds chloride → worsens acidosis · balanced fluids avoid it"),
("Renal 7","Which statement about ADH is INCORRECT?",["It is released from the posterior pituitary","It mainly controls body osmolarity","It changes water excretion, not solute","It increases sodium reabsorption via ENaC"],[3],"ENaC = aldosterone"),
