("Renal 8","A dog has hypoparathyroidism (no PTH). Which renal changes are expected?"+SA,["Less ECaC in the distal tubule","Less CYP27B1 activity","More claudin-14 activity","More phosphate reabsorption","Less calcium in the urine"],[0,1,2,3],"No PTH → nothing boosts ECaC/CYP27B1, nothing blocks claudin-14, nothing removes NaPi-IIa → more Ca²⁺ lost, phosphate kept"),
("Renal 8","Where is MOST filtered calcium reabsorbed, and is it hormone-regulated?",["Distal tubule; yes, by PTH","Proximal tubule; no, it's passive","Thick ascending limb; yes, by calcitonin","Collecting duct; yes, by vitamin D"],[1],"~65% passive (lumen-positive #1) · the distal 8% is the regulated part"),
("Renal 8","Which pairing of source and kidney effect is correct for FGF23?",["Parathyroid; adds NaPi-IIa","Osteocytes; adds NaPi-IIa","Parathyroid; removes NaPi-IIa","Osteocytes; removes NaPi-IIa"],[3],"Bone's phosphate alarm → phosphate dumped"),
("Renal 8","Which is a CALCITONIN effect in the kidney?",["Blocks claudin-14 in the thick ascending limb","Blocks claudin-16 in the thick ascending limb","Adds ECaC in the distal tubule","Adds calbindin in the distal tubule"],[1],"Calcitonin blocks the DOOR (claudin-16) · PTH blocks the BLOCKER (claudin-14)"),
# ---- Renal 9
("Renal 9","A dehydrated dog with low blood volume is also hypokalemic. Why does it lose very little potassium in the urine?",["Aldosterone is suppressed by low volume","Low tubular flow keeps BK channels closed","Insulin pulls potassium into muscle","Alpha cells secrete potassium into the lumen"],[1],"Aldosterone is HIGH for volume, but low flow means BK stays off and ROMK equilibrates"),
("Renal 9","On a very low-potassium diet, which cell adds potassium reabsorption, and how?",["Principal cell, through ROMK","Alpha-intercalated cell, through the H⁺/K⁺ pump","Beta-intercalated cell, through BK","Proximal tubule cell, through NKCC"],[1],"Lets the kidney keep up to ~98% of filtered K⁺"),
("Renal 9","Na⁺ 150, Cl⁻ 105, HCO₃⁻ 15. Anion gap and best interpretation?",["30; high gap, an acid was added","30; normal gap, bicarbonate was lost","15; normal gap, bicarbonate was lost","15; high gap, an acid was added"],[0],"150 − (105 + 15) = 30 → GOLD MARK"),
("Renal 9","Acidosis, normal anion gap, K⁺ 6.6 (high), urine pH 5.1, normal creatinine, no glucosuria. Most likely?",["Type 1 renal tubular acidosis","Type 2 renal tubular acidosis","Type 4 renal tubular acidosis","Late chronic kidney disease"],[2],"High K⁺ + can still acidify = low aldosterone signaling"),
("Renal 9","Which are features of TYPE 1 (distal) renal tubular acidosis?"+SA,["Low blood potassium","Urine pH above 5.5","Normal anion gap","Hyperchloremia","Glucosuria with normal blood glucose"],[0,1,2,3],"Glucosuria = type 2 (proximal/Fanconi)"),
("Renal 9","Why is ammonia the kidney's main buffer in CHRONIC acidosis?",["It is filtered in larger amounts than phosphate","The kidney can make more of it from glutamine","It is secreted directly as H⁺ by alpha cells","It raises blood pH by binding bicarbonate"],[1],"Phosphate is limited to what's filtered · ammonia production can be ramped up"),
("Renal 9","Which acidosis has a NORMAL anion gap?",["Lactic acidosis from shock","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Salicylate toxicity","Hyperchloremia from 0.9% NaCl"],[3],"Nothing new added; chloride displaced bicarbonate"),
("Renal 9","An acidotic patient has a potassium at the LOW end of normal. What does this suggest?",["Total body potassium is likely depleted","Potassium handling is completely normal","Insulin levels must be very high","Aldosterone must be absent"],[0],"Acidosis should push K⁺ UP · a low-normal value means the body is actually short (type 1 RTA horse)"),
